.SH NAME
mtr\-ng \- A modern implementation of mtr (My Traceroute) with unicode and terminal graphics
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmtr\-ng\fR [\fB\-c\fR|\fB\-\-count\fR] [\fB\-i\fR|\fB\-\-interval\fR] [\fB\-M\fR|\fB\-\-max\-hops\fR] [\fB\-s\fR|\fB\-\-psize\fR] [\fB\-4 \fR] [\fB\-6 \fR] [\fB\-r\fR|\fB\-\-report\fR] [\fB\-j\fR|\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-C\fR|\fB\-\-csv\fR] [\fB\-\-dot\fR] [\fB\-\-mermaid\fR] [\fB\-\-geojson\fR] [\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-numeric\fR] [\fB\-z\fR|\fB\-\-aslookup\fR] [\fB\-g\fR|\fB\-\-geo\fR] [\fB\-\-ipmap\fR] [\fB\-\-geoip\-mmdb\fR] [\fB\-\-privacy\-hops\fR] [\fB\-\-origin\fR] [\fB\-e\fR|\fB\-\-mpls\fR] [\fB\-\-flows\fR] [\fB\-\-pmtu\fR] [\fB\-\-sparkline\-scale\fR] [\fB\-\-ema\-alpha\fR] [\fB\-\-fields\fR] [\fB\-\-show\-all\fR] [\fB\-\-simulate\fR] [\fB\-P\fR|\fB\-\-protocol\fR] [\fB\-\-metrics\fR] [\fB\-\-record\fR] [\fB\-\-replay\fR] [\fB\-\-replay\-speed\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-\-log\-file\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] [\fITARGET\fR] 
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH DESCRIPTION
A modern implementation of mtr (My Traceroute) with unicode and terminal graphics
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-count\fR \fI<COUNT>\fR
Number of rounds to run (default: until quit; 10 in report mode, unless \-\-metrics)
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-interval\fR \fI<INTERVAL>\fR [default: 1000]
Time between rounds in milliseconds (at least 100, except with \-\-simulate)
.TP
\fB\-M\fR, \fB\-\-max\-hops\fR \fI<MAX_HOPS>\fR [default: 30]
Maximum number of hops (1\-255)
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-psize\fR \fI<BYTES>\fR [default: 64]
Size of each probe in bytes, IP header included, as in mtr (28\-1500). A probe never gets smaller than its headers and the bytes that tell it apart. Some MPLS routers don\*(Aqt answer much smaller probes than the default
.TP
\fB\-4\fR
Use IPv4 only
.TP
\fB\-6\fR
Use IPv6 only
.TP
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-\-report\fR
Enable report mode (non\-interactive)
.TP
\fB\-j\fR, \fB\-\-json\fR
Report as JSON, with mtr\*(Aqs field names (implies \-\-report)
.TP
\fB\-C\fR, \fB\-\-csv\fR
Report as CSV, one row per hop (implies \-\-report)
.TP
\fB\-\-dot\fR
Print the routes as a Graphviz graph instead of a table (implies \-\-report). Most useful with \-\-flows
.TP
\fB\-\-mermaid\fR
Print the routes as a Mermaid flowchart, which Markdown on GitHub, GitLab and many wikis draws (implies \-\-report). Most useful with \-\-flows
.TP
\fB\-\-geojson\fR
Print the located hops and the path between them as GeoJSON, for map tools (implies \-\-report; needs a geolocation database, see \-\-geo)
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-numeric\fR
Show IP addresses instead of hostnames
.TP
\fB\-z\fR, \fB\-\-aslookup\fR
Show the AS number of each hop. Asks Team Cymru\*(Aqs DNS about every public address
.TP
\fB\-g\fR, \fB\-\-geo\fR
Show where each hop is (Location and Dist columns), from a local geolocation database: nothing is sent anywhere. A location farther away than light in fibre gets in the hop\*(Aqs best RTT is marked with !
.TP
\fB\-\-ipmap\fR
Also ask RIPE IPmap where each public address that answers is, and trust its answers that rest on measurements. Sends those addresses to the RIPE NCC, which may measure them from RIPE Atlas probes. Works without a local database; shows the Location and Dist columns like \-g
.TP
\fB\-\-geoip\-mmdb\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Geolocation database in MaxMind\*(Aqs format: DB\-IP City Lite or GeoLite2 City. Default: the newest .mmdb in ~/.local/share/mtr\-ng, then the usual system places
.TP
\fB\-\-privacy\-hops\fR \fI<N>\fR [default: 0]
Hide the first N hops: their addresses, names, ASes and locations are neither shown, in the TUI or any report, nor looked up. For screenshots and reports that shouldn\*(Aqt give away the network they were taken from
.TP
\fB\-\-origin\fR \fI<LAT,LON>\fR
Where you are, in degrees, for distances and the plausibility check. Default: the first hop the database places in a city, with its uncertainty
.TP
\fB\-e\fR, \fB\-\-mpls\fR
Show the MPLS labels (RFC 4950) and interfaces (RFC 5837) routers report, under their hop
.TP
\fB\-\-flows\fR \fI<N|auto>\fR [default: 1]
Probe this many flows in turn, one per round. All probes of a flow look alike to load balancers (Paris traceroute), so a flow follows one path; several flows reveal the paths a load balancer spreads traffic over. With auto, new flows until every branch is found with 95% confidence (MDA\-Lite), then the fewest flows that pass every branch
.TP
\fB\-\-pmtu\fR
Find the path MTU and where it narrows: each round one more probe, as large as the path seems to allow, with Don\*(Aqt Fragment set
.TP
\fB\-\-sparkline\-scale\fR \fI<SPARKLINE_SCALE>\fR [default: logarithmic]
Sparkline scaling mode: linear or logarithmic (default: logarithmic)
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
linear
.IP \(bu 2
logarithmic
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-ema\-alpha\fR \fI<EMA_ALPHA>\fR [default: 0.1]
Smoothing factor for the EMA column, above 0 and at most 1. Higher follows recent RTTs faster
.TP
\fB\-\-fields\fR \fI<FIELDS>\fR
Select which columns to display (default: hop,host,loss,sent,last,avg,ema,best,worst,graph)
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
hop: Hop number
.IP \(bu 2
host: Hostname/IP address
.IP \(bu 2
asn: Origin AS number (looked up with \-\-aslookup)
.IP \(bu 2
as\-name: The AS\*(Aqs name, its handle as the registry has it (looked up with the ASN)
.IP \(bu 2
prefix: The announced prefix the address is in (looked up with the ASN)
.IP \(bu 2
geo: Where the hop is, from the geolocation database (see \-\-geo)
.IP \(bu 2
dist: Distance from the origin in km (see \-\-origin)
.IP \(bu 2
stretch: The best RTT past the origin\*(Aqs over light in fibre\*(Aqs for the distance: 1 is a straight fibre
.IP \(bu 2
reach: Farthest from here a reply can come in the best RTT, in km
.IP \(bu 2
back: Routers the reply crossed, from its TTL; +N or \-N when the way there was shorter or longer
.IP \(bu 2
loss: Packet loss percentage
.IP \(bu 2
sent: Number of packets sent
.IP \(bu 2
last: Last RTT measurement
.IP \(bu 2
avg: Average RTT
.IP \(bu 2
ema: Exponential moving average RTT
.IP \(bu 2
jitter: Last jitter value
.IP \(bu 2
jitter\-avg: Average jitter
.IP \(bu 2
best: Best (minimum) RTT
.IP \(bu 2
worst: Worst (maximum) RTT
.IP \(bu 2
stdev: Standard deviation of the RTT
.IP \(bu 2
graph: RTT sparkline graph
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-show\-all\fR
Display all available columns
.TP
\fB\-\-simulate\fR
Show fake data instead of probing (for demos and testing); marked SIMULATED
.TP
\fB\-P\fR, \fB\-\-protocol\fR \fI<PROTOCOL>\fR [default: icmp]
Probe protocol (tcp is not implemented yet)
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
icmp: ICMP Echo (ping) \- default and most common
.IP \(bu 2
udp: UDP datagrams to port 33434 and up, one port per flow
.IP \(bu 2
tcp: TCP SYN probes (not implemented yet)
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-metrics\fR \fI<[ADDR:]PORT>\fR
Serve the statistics for Prometheus at http://ADDR/metrics while tracing. A bare port listens on 127.0.0.1. With \-\-report and no \-\-count, mtr\-ng runs until stopped, then prints the report
.TP
\fB\-\-record\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Write what the trace sees to PATH as it happens, one JSON object per line, for \-\-replay. PATH must not exist yet
.TP
\fB\-\-replay\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Play back a \-\-record log instead of probing: in the TUI at the pace it was recorded (see \-\-replay\-speed), with \-\-report all at once. Sends nothing
.TP
\fB\-\-replay\-speed\fR \fI<X>\fR [default: 1]
Play back this many times faster than recorded
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Report mode: print errors only, no warnings
.TP
\fB\-\-log\-file\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Append debug logs to this file (level can be overridden with RUST_LOG)
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.TP
[\fITARGET\fR] [default: ]
Target hostname or IP address
