.SH COLUMNS
Pick them with \fB\-\-fields\fR, or with \fBo\fR while running. Times are in milliseconds.
.TP
.B hop
Hop number
.TP
.B host
Host name or address
.TP
.B asn
Autonomous system
.TP
.B as\-name
Autonomous system's name
.TP
.B prefix
Announced prefix
.TP
.B geo
Location (city, country)
.TP
.B dist
Distance from the origin, km
.TP
.B stretch
RTT over light in fibre's
.TP
.B reach
Farthest the best RTT reaches, km
.TP
.B back
Routers on the way back
.TP
.B loss
Packet loss
.TP
.B sent
Probes sent
.TP
.B last
Last RTT
.TP
.B avg
Average RTT
.TP
.B ema
Smoothed RTT (EMA)
.TP
.B jitter
Last jitter
.TP
.B jitter\-avg
Average jitter
.TP
.B best
Best RTT
.TP
.B worst
Worst RTT
.TP
.B stdev
RTT standard deviation
.TP
.B graph
RTT history graph
.PP
The back column counts the routers a reply crossed from the TTL it arrived with, assuming it left with the next common initial TTL up (32, 64, 128 or 255). When that differs from the routers the probe crossed on the way there, the difference follows: 9 +2 is nine routers back, two more than there, so the reply came back another way. Routers that send replies with an unusual TTL, and MPLS tunnels that hide their routers, skew the count.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B mtr\-ng example.com
Trace interactively until q.
.TP
.B mtr\-ng \-r example.com
Print a report after 10 rounds.
.TP
.B mtr\-ng \-r \-c 100 \-n 1.1.1.1
A 100\-round report without DNS.
.TP
.B mtr\-ng \-\-fields hop,host,loss,avg,graph example.com
Show fewer columns.
.TP
.B mtr\-ng \-i 500 example.com
Probe every 500 ms.
.TP
.B mtr\-ng \-r \-c 40 \-\-flows 8 example.com
Follow 8 flows and show which router each met: load balancers split them.
.TP
.B mtr\-ng \-r \-c 40 \-P udp \-\-flows 8 example.com
The same with UDP probes, whose ports load balancers hash more often than ICMP fields.
.TP
.B mtr\-ng \-r \-P udp \-\-flows auto example.com
Find every load\-balanced branch, 95% sure, then follow one flow per branch. See LOAD BALANCING.
.TP
.B mtr\-ng \-r \-\-fields hop,host,back,loss,avg example.com
Compare each reply's way back with the probe's way there.
.TP
.B mtr\-ng \-c 40 \-\-flows 8 \-\-dot example.com | dot \-Tsvg > routes.svg
Draw the routes with Graphviz.
.TP
.B mtr\-ng \-c 40 \-\-flows 8 \-\-mermaid example.com
The routes as a Mermaid flowchart, for Markdown on GitHub or GitLab.
.TP
.B mtr\-ng \-r \-e example.com
Show MPLS labels and the interfaces routers name (RFC 5837) under their hop.
.TP
.B mtr\-ng \-\-record trace.ndjson example.com
Trace interactively and keep a log of every probe, reply and lookup.
.TP
.B mtr\-ng \-\-replay trace.ndjson \-\-replay\-speed 10
Play the log back in the TUI ten times as fast; with \-r, print its report at once. See RECORD AND REPLAY.
.TP
.B mtr\-ng \-r \-n \-\-metrics 9819 example.com
Trace until stopped, for Prometheus to scrape at http://127.0.0.1:9819/metrics. See METRICS.
.TP
.B mtr\-ng \-r \-c 30 \-\-pmtu example.com
Find the path MTU and the hop where it narrows.
.TP
.B mtr\-ng \-g example.com
Show where each hop is; m draws the path on a map. See GEOLOCATION.
.TP
.B mtr\-ng \-r \-g \-\-origin 52.37,4.89 example.com
Measure distances from Amsterdam, which also tightens the light\-speed check.
.TP
.B mtr\-ng \-r \-\-ipmap example.com
Also place the hops with RIPE IPmap, which measures where routers are. See GEOLOCATION.
.TP
.B mtr\-ng \-c 20 \-\-geojson example.com > path.geojson
Save the located hops and the path for a map viewer such as geojson.io.
.TP
.B mtr\-ng \-r \-\-privacy\-hops 2 example.com
A report to share: hops 1 and 2 are hidden and never looked up. See PRIVACY.
.TP
.B mtr\-ng \-\-simulate example.com
Fake data, marked SIMULATED.
.SH LOAD BALANCING
With \-\-flows auto, each round sends a new flow until every hop that answers has answered enough flows to rule out a branch no flow met: 6 flows where one address answered, 11 where two did, 16 where three did, and 63 for eleven, the most 64 flows can confirm. That leaves less than a 5% chance per hop of a missed branch, if load balancers spread flows evenly (MDA\-Lite). Then the fewest flows that together pass every address take turns, so each branch keeps its own statistics, and every ten minutes the search starts over. A note under each balanced hop gives its branches; a report without \-c runs until the search is done, and ends with what it found. Where branches cross between hops, one address leading to several and another reached from several, a branch may have been missed: MDA\-Lite assumes they don't cross.
.SH NAT
A router that drops a probe quotes the probe's IP header and first eight bytes in its ICMP error. A NAT that changed the probe ought to translate that quote back too (RFC 5508), and Linux's NAT does, address, port and checksum alike, so most home routers stay out of sight. Some NATs leave the quote as the probe was past them. Then it shows the probe leaving from the NAT's address, or, for an IPv4 echo request, with the checksum the NAT gave it when it changed the identifier, which Dublin traceroute also checks. mtr\-ng notes NAT before this hop under the first hop past each such NAT. A UDP probe's checksum is its sequence number, so past such a NAT mtr\-ng takes the NAT's change off the checksum again, using the address and port in the quote. On Linux without privileges, ping sockets hand back the probe as it was sent instead of the quote, so no NAT shows.
.SH GEOLOCATION
With \-g, the geo, dist or stretch columns, or the map (m), mtr\-ng places hops with a local database in MaxMind's format: \-\-geoip\-mmdb, else the newest .mmdb in ~/.local/share/mtr\-ng, else dbip\-city\-lite.mmdb or GeoLite2\-City.mmdb in /usr/share/GeoIP, /var/lib/GeoIP or Homebrew's var/GeoIP. Lookups never leave the machine, and private addresses are never looked up. DB\-IP's free City Lite database (CC BY 4.0) can be fetched with:
.PP
.nf
mkdir \-p ~/.local/share/mtr\-ng && curl \-fL "https://download.db\-ip.com/free/dbip\-city\-lite\-$(date +%Y\-%m).mmdb.gz" | gunzip > ~/.local/share/mtr\-ng/dbip.part && mv ~/.local/share/mtr\-ng/dbip.part ~/.local/share/mtr\-ng/dbip\-city\-lite.mmdb
.fi
.PP
A database places a router where its owner registered it, which can be a continent away. Where a router's name carries its network's city code, as Arelion's adm\-b3\-link.ip.twelve99.net does, that city goes before the database; mtr\-ng knows the codes of Arelion, Cogent, Hurricane Electric and Lumen. Either way, a reply cannot come from farther than light in fibre travels in its round trip, about 100 km per ms, so a location farther from the origin than the hop's best RTT allows, granting both locations their accuracy, is marked ! and left off the map.
.PP
Distances are measured from \-\-origin, or else from the first hop placed in a city. The probing host is then somewhere within that hop's reach, which widens the margin. The stretch column is a hop's best RTT, less that hop's, over the round trip light in fibre needs for the distance: 1.0 is a straight fibre, most paths take 2 or 3. The report and the map also compare the path through the located hops with the great circle.
.PP
\-\-ipmap also asks RIPE IPmap (ipmap.ripe.net) where each public address that answers is, with or without a database. IPmap measures routers from nearby RIPE Atlas probes and scores each answer; mtr\-ng takes city\-level answers that score 30 or more, which rest on measurements, and none for anycast addresses. Such an answer replaces the database's location, but not a city from the router's name, nor when the RTT rules it out and not the database's location. See PRIVACY.
.SH RECORD AND REPLAY
\-\-record PATH writes what the trace sees to PATH as it happens, one JSON object per line: first what was traced, from where and how, then each probe sent, each reply (from whom, its RTT, TTL and MPLS labels) or timeout, and each name and AS that arrived, with the seconds since the start. PATH must not exist yet. \-\-replay PATH plays a log back into the TUI at the pace it was recorded, or \-\-replay\-speed times faster, and with \-\-report prints the report at once; it sends nothing and looks nothing up, and any report format works. The path MTU search and the branch search of \-\-flows auto are not recorded. Hops hidden by \-\-privacy\-hops are recorded hidden, so a log can be passed on like a report. A trace of an hour at one round a second takes about 10 MB.
.SH METRICS
\-\-metrics [ADDR:]PORT serves the trace at http://ADDR:PORT/metrics while it runs, in OpenMetrics to scrapers that ask for it, as Prometheus does, and in the Prometheus text format to the rest. A bare port listens on 127.0.0.1. With \-\-report and no \-\-count, mtr\-ng runs until stopped by Ctrl\-C or SIGTERM, then prints the report. Per hop there are counters of probes sent, received and lost (mtr_ng_hop_sent_total and so on), the loss ratio, a summary of the round\-trip times (mtr_ng_hop_rtt_seconds, with quantiles over the last 600 probes and sum and count over all), the last, best and worst RTT, its standard deviation and jitter, and per address that answered an info metric with its name and AS (mtr_ng_hop_responder_info) and a counter of its replies. Every sample is labelled with the target. Ports below 1024 need privileges mtr\-ng has given up by then.
.SH PRIVACY
Apart from the probes, mtr\-ng sends the target's name and, unless \-n, the name of every address that answers to this machine's resolver, and with \-z every public address that answers to Team Cymru's DNS service. Locations come from a local database, and with \-\-ipmap every public address that answers also goes to RIPE IPmap, which may measure it from RIPE Atlas probes. Private addresses are never looked up anywhere. \-\-privacy\-hops N hides the first N hops in the TUI and every report format, with the local host name, and never looks them up; branches among them stay apart, and they are hidden from \-\-metrics too. With \-\-metrics, anyone who can reach its address can read the trace. The a key lists what the running trace sends, and the data sources with their licences.
.SH PRIVILEGES
On macOS mtr\-ng needs no privileges: it reads the routers' answers with an unprivileged ICMP socket, for ICMP and UDP probes alike. On Linux, ICMP works without privileges through a ping socket when the user's group is in net.ipv4.ping_group_range, as every group is on systemd 243 and newer, but a ping socket shows no MPLS labels. UDP probes and MPLS labels need root or the CAP_NET_RAW capability (setcap cap_net_raw+ep "$(command \-v mtr\-ng)"), which the Debian package grants on install. FreeBSD has no unprivileged ICMP socket, so mtr\-ng needs root there: run it with doas or sudo, or install it setuid root. mtr\-ng opens its raw sockets first and then gives up these privileges before it resolves the target or reads a packet: a setuid install becomes the user who ran it, and root keeps only the right to read files. On Windows mtr\-ng needs no administrator either: it sends echo requests through Windows' ICMP API, which builds them itself, so there are no flows, UDP probes, MPLS labels or \-\-pmtu. TCP probes are not implemented yet.
